<details class="search-help">
  <summary>CSV and JSON format</summary>

  <p class="muted">
    Both formats round-trip with export, so the easiest way to see the shape is
    to export a collection first. Recognised fields: <code>type</code>,
    <code>title</code>, <code>system</code>, <code>author</code>,
    <code>min_players</code>, <code>max_players</code>, <code>completed</code>,
    <code>evergreen</code>, <code>local_multiplayer</code>,
    <code>online_multiplayer</code>, <code>cooperative</code>,
    <code>competitive</code>, <code>labels</code>, and <code>notes</code>.
    Only <code>title</code> is required; <code>labels</code> is a
    comma-separated list (CSV) or an array (JSON) matched to your existing
    labels by name.
  </p>

  <p class="muted">CSV (a header row is required):</p>
  <pre class="code-block">Type,Title,System,Completed,Labels
video_game,Hades,Switch,true,"Comfort games, Backlog"
book,Dune,,true,</pre>

  <p class="muted">JSON (an array of objects):</p>
  <pre class="code-block">[
  { "type": "video_game", "title": "Hades", "system": "Switch", "completed": true },
  { "type": "book", "title": "Dune", "author": "Frank Herbert" }
]</pre>
</details>
